NNAAMMEE
     lloocckkssttaatt — report kernel lock and profiling statistics

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     lloocckkssttaatt [--AACCEEHHIIVV] [--ee _e_v_e_n_t_-_l_i_s_t] [--ii _r_a_t_e] [--bb | --tt | --hh | --ss _d_e_p_t_h]
              [--nn _n_u_m_-_r_e_c_o_r_d_s] [--ll _l_o_c_k [,_s_i_z_e]] [--dd _d_u_r_a_t_i_o_n]
              [--ff _f_u_n_c_t_i_o_n [,_s_i_z_e]] [--TT] [--kkggwwWWRRppPP] [--DD _c_o_u_n_t] [--oo --ffiilleennaammee]
              [--xx _o_p_t [=_v_a_l]] _c_o_m_m_a_n_d [[_a_r_g_s]]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The lloocckkssttaatt utility gathers and displays kernel locking and profiling
     statistics.  lloocckkssttaatt allows you to specify which events to watch (for
     example, spin on adaptive mutex, block on read access to rwlock due to
     waiting writers, and so forth), how much data to gather for each event,
     and how to display the data.  By default, lloocckkssttaatt monitors all lock con‐
     tention events, gathers frequency and timing data about those events, and
     displays the data in decreasing frequency order, so that the most common
     events appear first.

     lloocckkssttaatt gathers data until the specified command completes.  For exam‐
     ple, to gather statistics for a fixed-time interval, use sleep(1) as the
     command, as follows:

           # lockstat sleep 5

     When the --II option is specified, lloocckkssttaatt establishes a per-processor
     high-level periodic interrupt source to gather profiling data.  The in‐
     terrupt handler simply generates a lloocckkssttaatt event whose caller is the in‐
     terrupted PC (program counter).  The profiling event is just like any
     other lloocckkssttaatt event, so all of the normal lloocckkssttaatt options are applica‐
     ble.

     lloocckkssttaatt relies on DTrace to modify the running kernel's text to inter‐
     cept events of interest.  This imposes a small but measurable overhead on
     all system activity, so access to lloocckkssttaatt is restricted to super-user by
     default.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
     The following options are supported:

     --VV      Print the D program used to gather the requested data.

   EEvveenntt SSeelleeccttiioonn
     If no event selection options are specified, the default is --CC.

     --AA      Watch all lock events.  --AA is equivalent to --CCHH.

     --CC      Watch contention events.

     --EE      Watch error events.

     --ee _e_v_e_n_t_-_l_i_s_t
             Only watch the specified events.  _e_v_e_n_t_-_l_i_s_t is a comma-separated
             list of events or ranges of events such as 1,4-7,35.  Run
             lloocckkssttaatt with no arguments to get a brief description of all
             events.

     --HH      Watch hold events.

     --II      Watch profiling interrupt events.

     --ii _r_a_t_e
             Interrupt rate (per second) for --II.  The default is 97 Hz, so
             that profiling doesn't run in lockstep with the clock interrupt
             (which runs at 100 Hz).

