HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The lloocckkssttaatt utility first appeared in FreeBSD 7.1.

NNOOTTEESS
     Tail-call elimination can affect call sites.  For example, if ffoooo()+0x50
     calls bbaarr() and the last thing bbaarr() does is call mmttxx__uunnlloocckk(), the com‐
     piler can arrange for bbaarr() to branch to mmttxx__uunnlloocckk() with a return ad‐
     dress of ffoooo()+0x58. Thus, the mmttxx__uunnlloocckk() in bbaarr() will appear as
     though it occurred at ffoooo()+0x58.

     The PC in the stack frame in which an interrupt occurs can be bogus be‐
     cause, between function calls, the compiler is free to use the return ad‐
     dress register for local storage.

     When using the --II and --ss options together, the interrupted PC will usu‐
     ally not appear anywhere in the stack since the interrupt handler is en‐
     tered asynchronously, not by a function call from that PC.

